# NSG spike — can 5G-LENA `nr` talk to our RIC? Partly, and now we know the cost.
#
# Ran before committing weeks to an nr engine profile. The premise under test:
# the nr module is the route to TDD slot patterns, MIMO/rank, carrier
# aggregation, per-cell carriers and real UL — all of which the mmWave module
# structurally cannot express — and its E2 scaffolding looked already wired.
#
# Changes live in the ns-3 tree (Ubuntu-2004-nsoran), not in this repo:
#   src/nr/model/nr-gnb-net-device.{h,cc}
#   src/nr/model/nr-gnb-rrc.h
#   src/nr/helper/nr-helper.cc
#   scratch/orange-rf-channel-reconfiguration.cc
# Reproduce with /root/nsoran/nr_kpm_spike.sh in that distro.
#
# ── ANSWERED: nr establishes a real E2 association ─────────────────────────
# Scenario log, verbatim:
#     [SCTP] Binding client socket with source port 38471
#     [SCTP] Connecting to server at 127.0.0.1:36421 ...
#     [SCTP] Connection established
#     [SCTP] Sent E2-SETUP-REQUEST
#     [E2AP] Received SETUP-RESPONSE-SUCCESS
# and the RIC counted the node (E2 SETUP-REQUESTs: 0 -> 1). So an nr gNB CAN be
# an E2 node on the same FlexRIC the mmWave engine uses, with the same service
# models loaded. The premise holds at the association layer.
#
# ── NOT ANSWERED: no KPM indication observed yet ───────────────────────────
# The run aborts before the xApp subscribes, so BuildAndSendReportMessage has
# never actually fired. The spike de-risked the connection, not the payload.
#
# ── Three upstream defects found and fixed to get that far ─────────────────
# 1. `BuildAndSendReportMessage` DID NOT EXIST. KpmSubscriptionCallback carried
#    `//BuildAndSendReportMessage (params);` — a commented-out call to a
#    function nobody ever wrote. Implemented minimally here: a CU-CP indication
#    carrying the active-UE count, built with MmWaveIndicationMessageHelper,
#    which lives in contrib/oran-interface and NOT in the mmwave module, so nr
#    can use it without depending on mmwave. NrGnbRrc also needed a public
#    GetNumberOfUes() — m_ueMap was private with no accessor, so the E2 layer
#    had no way to count UEs.
# 2. `E2Termination::Start` WAS NEVER CALLED. NrHelper creates the termination
#    and attaches it as an attribute; nothing ever started it, so the object
#    existed, the callbacks were registered, and no SCTP association was opened.
#    The RIC therefore saw zero E2 nodes — and its own xApp path asserts on an
#    empty node list ("No global node conected??"), taking the RIC down. mmWave
#    schedules this in its DoInitialize; nr now does too.
# 3. THE SCENARIO TIED THE TWO E2 SWITCHES TOGETHER.
#    orange-rf-channel-reconfiguration.cc had `e2nrEnabled = enableE2FileLogging;`
#    — opposite switches. File logging ON: the device writes CSV and never sends
#    an indication. File logging OFF: that line also turned E2ModeNr off, so no
#    E2Termination was created at all. E2 was unreachable in BOTH directions,
#    which is why nobody noticed the path was hollow.
#
# ── One defect still open, and it is the next thing to fix ─────────────────
# With more than one gNB the run dies at +0.016 s:
#     [SCTP] Connection error: Cannot assign requested address
#     NS_ASSERT failed, cond="m_ptr", "Attempted to dereference zero pointer"
# Only ONE device logged "starting E2 termination", so the others reach
# DoInitialize with a null m_e2term — an ordering problem between NrHelper
# setting the attribute and the device initialising, not just a port clash.
# (The local port WAS also wrong — m_e2localPort + cellId collides when several
# devices share a cellId, as the multi-bandwidth-part scenarios do. Changed to a
# per-device counter. That was not the cause; it is still a latent bug.)
#
# ── Read on the cost, now evidence-based rather than a source read ─────────
# Cheaper than feared on the connection side: E2 association, service-model
# registration and subscription handling all work, and the message builders are
# shared contrib code rather than mmwave-private. More expensive than feared on
# the maturity side: three separate never-finished pieces in one path, plus a
# multi-gNB crash, before a single indication exists. Budget the nr profile as
# "finish someone else's half-written integration", not "wire up a working
# module".
#
# The order that follows from this: fix the multi-gNB E2 setup, see one
# indication end to end, THEN port the DU/CU-UP metrics from
# mmwave-enb-net-device.cc, THEN decide about the energy model (nr has none).
